# =========================================================================
# harness.bcyr — the measurement floor every other number sits on.
#
# `noop` is an empty call in a tight loop: whatever it reports is what the
# harness itself costs, and no benchmark in this tree can legitimately come in
# below it. It has been in `bench-history.csv` since 2026-07-31 and reads **2ns**,
# which is the direct-call cost `lib/bench.cyr:43` documents.
#
# ⚠ **This file was `tests/agnosai.bcyr` until 2026-08-10** — a `cyrius port`
# scaffold living in `tests/`, which is why an audit of `benches/*.bcyr` did not
# see it even while three files in that directory were failing to compile.
# CLAUDE.md says benches are flat in `benches/`; this now is. The bench NAME is
# unchanged, so its history is continuous.
#
# ✅ **The timer floor is measured by the harness now (cyrius 6.5.19), not
# declared** — `bench_clock_overhead_ns()` (lib/bench.cyr:141) calibrates one
# clock read at first use and `bench_batch_stop` subtracts it. This note used to
# warn that `lib/bench.cyr:6` documented `clock_gettime: ~120ns` while this host
# measured **1.32us** (2,000,000 iterations, `clock_epoch_secs_baseline` in
# `benches/fleet.bcyr`) — an 11x error that anything subtracting the constant
# inherited. agnosai filed it 2026-08-11 and it is fixed upstream; the constant
# is gone and a single number could never have replaced it, because the four
# release-gate hosts span 15ns to 3,550ns.
#
# ⚠ **What did NOT change here: every `.bcyr` in this tree already batched.**
# 6.5.19's other half re-sized `bench_run`, which used to wrap a clock pair
# around each iteration and floored 57 of 79 rows in *cyrius's own* history at
# ~2 clock reads. All ten agnosai bench files use
# `bench_batch_start`/`bench_batch_stop` and none calls `bench_run`, so that
# inflation never entered `bench-history.csv` and the series stays continuous
# across the bump. The floor subtraction is one clock read per BATCH, which at
# these iteration counts is below the reported integer ns.
#
# ⚠ **The floor moves between reboots** — the TSC-watchdog trip is per-boot, and
# this same box has recorded ~400ns and ~1,700ns. Cross-run comparison of micro
# rows is only sound within one boot. `bench_report` prints the floor it
# measured, so a row's provenance is recoverable from the run output.
#
# Stdlib is auto-prepended from cyrius.cyml — do NOT include stdlib modules.
# =========================================================================

# ────────────
# Every row in this file used to call `bench_batch_start`/`bench_batch_stop`
# EXACTLY ONCE, so `bench_batch_stop`'s per-op value was the only sample it ever
# saw and `min == max == avg` by construction — the dispersion column carried no
# information at all. `lib/bench.cyr`'s `bench_batch_stop` already folds each
# call's per-op figure into min/max, so N rounds give N real samples for free.
#
# ⚠ **Total work is UNCHANGED**: the inner loop bound and the batch size are both
# divided by `_BR`, so `rounds x batch` equals the old single batch. Integer
# division can shave a few iterations off a batch that does not divide evenly;
# `bench_iterations` reports the true accumulated count either way.
#
# ⚠ **This RE-BASELINES every row in this file.** `_bench_net` subtracts the
# measured clock floor once per `bench_batch_stop`, so five stops subtract it
# five times where one subtracted it once. Rows are not comparable across
# 2026-08-13. See roadmap D4.
var _BR = 5;

fn bench_noop(): i64 { return 0; }

fn main(): i64 {
    alloc_init();
    var b = bench_new("noop");
    # Hand-wrapped rather than by the D4 transform: the body is a `while`, not
    # the `for (var i = 0; i < n; ...)` shape the other 145 sites share.
    for (var _r = 0; _r < _BR; _r = _r + 1) {
        bench_batch_start(b);
        var i = 0;
        while (i < 1000000 / _BR) { bench_noop(); i = i + 1; }
        bench_batch_stop(b, 1000000 / _BR);
    }
    bench_report(b);
    return 0;
}

var r = main();
syscall(60, r);
